Elmi Guray Academy.

2
00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:04.497
Lesson 7.

3
00:00:04.497 --> 00:00:06.915
Learning with a Community.

4
00:00:06.915 --> 00:00:21.345
Lesson Overview Course AI Tools for Developers Lesson Lesson 7 Topic Learning with a Community Level Beginner Learning Objectives By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: Explain why learning with other people can be helpful.

5
00:00:21.345 --> 00:00:25.869
Identify useful activities inside a learning community.

6
00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:29.986
Ask technical questions clearly and respectfully.

7
00:00:29.986 --> 00:00:34.048
Support other learners without completing all their work.

8
00:00:34.048 --> 00:00:38.763
Share progress, ideas, and achievements responsibly.

9
00:00:38.763 --> 00:00:42.078
Follow good online-community behavior.

10
00:00:42.078 --> 00:00:48.899
Main principle: You can study independently, but you do not have to solve every problem alone.

11
00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:56.385
A respectful learning community can provide encouragement, explanations, feedback, and new ideas.

12
00:00:56.385 --> 00:01:02.133
English Technical Section Learning software development can sometimes feel difficult.

13
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:11.874
A learner may encounter errors, unfamiliar terminology, confusing instructions, or a problem that takes a long time to understand.

14
00:01:11.874 --> 00:01:17.907
A learning community gives students a place to communicate with people who have similar goals.

15
00:01:17.907 --> 00:01:26.249
Members can ask questions, discuss lessons, exchange ideas, celebrate achievements, and encourage one another.

16
00:01:26.249 --> 00:01:35.543
1 Ask Questions A community can help when you are stuck, but your question should contain enough information for others to understand the problem.

17
00:01:35.543 --> 00:01:40.611
A useful technical question may include: What you are trying to do.

18
00:01:40.611 --> 00:01:43.288
What you expected to happen.

19
00:01:43.288 --> 00:01:45.665
What actually happened.

20
00:01:45.665 --> 00:01:48.301
The exact error message.

21
00:01:48.301 --> 00:01:51.263
The relevant code or screenshot.

22
00:01:51.263 --> 00:01:53.790
What you have already tried.

23
00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:59.782
2 Learn from Other Questions You can learn even when you are not the person asking the question.

24
00:01:59.782 --> 00:02:05.801
Reading another learner's problem and solution may teach you how to avoid the same mistake.

25
00:02:05.801 --> 00:02:12.160
Community discussions can also introduce different ways to solve the same programming problem.

26
00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:21.644
3 Share Your Progress Sharing a completed lesson, small project, certificate, or difficult problem you solved can strengthen your motivation.

27
00:02:21.644 --> 00:02:25.597
Progress does not need to be perfect or dramatic.

28
00:02:25.597 --> 00:02:30.951
Completing one lesson or fixing one error is still meaningful progress.

29
00:02:30.951 --> 00:02:37.527
4 Help Other Learners Explaining a concept to another person can improve your own understanding.

30
00:02:37.527 --> 00:02:40.937
Good help guides the learner toward the answer.

31
00:02:40.937 --> 00:02:44.877
It does not simply complete every assignment for them.

32
00:02:44.877 --> 00:02:54.605
5 Receive Feedback Other learners may notice unclear code, design problems, missing instructions, or mistakes that you did not see.

33
00:02:54.605 --> 00:03:02.105
Feedback should be specific, respectful, and focused on improving the work rather than criticizing the person.

34
00:03:02.105 --> 00:03:10.067
6 Stay Motivated Seeing others learn and make progress can remind you that difficulty is a normal part of development.

35
00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:16.413
A supportive community can encourage you to continue when a lesson or project feels challenging.

36
00:03:16.413 --> 00:03:22.540
How to Ask a Good Community Question Avoid questions that contain no useful detail.

37
00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:25.054
My code does not work.

38
00:03:25.054 --> 00:03:26.684
Help me.

39
00:03:26.684 --> 00:03:32.880
A clearer question would be: I am trying to open the registration page from the Home screen.

40
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.369
Expected result: Tapping the Register button should open /register.

41
00:03:38.369 --> 00:03:42.567
Actual result: Nothing happens when I tap the button.

42
00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:51.480
File: src/app/index.tsx What I tried: I restarted Expo and refreshed the browser.

43
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.271
Please help me identify the navigation problem.

44
00:03:55.271 --> 00:04:01.630
The second question makes it easier for another learner or instructor to understand the problem.

45
00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:06.317
How to Give Helpful Feedback Weak feedback: Your code is bad.

46
00:04:06.317 --> 00:04:12.445
Better feedback: The form works, but the labels are difficult to read on a small screen.

47
00:04:12.445 --> 00:04:17.894
Consider increasing the label size and adding more space between the fields.

48
00:04:17.894 --> 00:04:24.728
Good feedback explains the issue and suggests a possible improvement without disrespecting the learner.

